WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.380
Directors Joe and Anthony Russo combined arguably
the most confusing element of Avengers: Endgame

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:08.240
with one of the most heart-wrenching moments
in the last few minutes of the film.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.300
Chris Evans' Steve Rogers returned from his
final time travel mission as an old man, gifted

00:00:12.309 --> 00:00:16.290
his shield to Anthony Mackie's Sam Wilson,
and kept coy about his wedding band…that

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:19.360
we all know was a sign he got married to Hayley
Atwell's Peggy Carter.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.980
Once the fuzzy feelings that the scene inspired
faded, though, fans were left scratching their

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:27.619
heads and wondering how exactly Old Man Cap
got back to the main timeline, as his appearance

00:00:27.619 --> 00:00:31.539
seemingly contradicts the rules of time travel
as previously outlined in the movie.

00:00:31.539 --> 00:00:35.420
Finally, more than three months after viewers
first watched Endgame up on the big screen,

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:36.660
we have the definitive answer.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.560
The Russo Brothers recently held an Ask Me
Anything session on Reddit, where they detailed

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:44.410
how the grizzled Captain America found his
way back to the main timeline without completely

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:48.380
shattering the established time travel theory
the Ancient One stated early on in Endgame.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:52.719
A fan asked the Russos how Cap came back to
the present after returning the Infinity Stones

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:56.350
to their respective places in the past, and
requested some clarification on the rules

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:57.220
of time travel.

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:00.660
According to the Ancient One, when someone
takes a Stone from its original place and

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:04.300
travels through time, they create a branched
timeline that doesn't alter the past.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:08.320
When Steve Rogers returned the Stones following
the big battle against Thanos, he wore a quantum

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.060
suit and used a Pym Particle to travel back
in time.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:14.560
But when the older Steve appeared on the park
bench at the end of Endgame, he wasn't in

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:15.480
a quantum suit.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:18.799
Did Cap figure out a way to come back to the
present without using the same method that

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:19.800
sent him to the past?

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:23.420
Did he live out his life with Peggy in the
main timeline, thus creating two separate

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:27.022
Captain Americas and countering the rules
of time travel, or did he marry her in an

00:01:27.022 --> 00:01:29.140
alternate timeline and keep everything intact?

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:33.200
The Russos confirmed on Reddit that Cap, quote,
"would have had to have worn the quantum suit"

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:37.430
and used a Pym Particle to re-join the present
timeline, just as he needed to do to jump

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:38.280
back to the past.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:42.160
But the reason why he isn't sporting the silvery
suit on the bench is because, quite simply,

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:43.210
he took it off earlier.

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:46.940
According to the Russos, quote, "that is not
the exact moment to which he returned."

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:50.740
So, there you have it: Cap did indeed return
to the main timeline using the quantum suit

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:55.180
and a Pym Particle, but he was hanging around
in the year 2023 as a wrinkly, white-haired

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:58.980
old man at least long enough to change out
of the suit and into something your bird-watching

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:00.140
grandfather would wear.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:01.560
"Time travel!"

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:06.140
This explanation indicates that Joe and Anthony
Russo are of the opinion that Steve and Peggy

00:02:06.149 --> 00:02:10.110
reunited, got married, and shared a full and
happy life filled with many evenings spent

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:13.720
dancing in their living room all in a timeline
alternate to the main one of the Marvel Cinematic

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:14.440
Universe.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:17.440
Joe Russo even told Entertainment Weekly in
May 2019,

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:21.280
"If Cap were to go back into the past and
live there, he would create a branched reality."

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:24.620
However, not everyone involved with Endgame
agrees with that assessment.

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:28.540
Screenwriters Christopher Markus and Stephen
McFeely have maintained their belief that

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:32.590
Steve's grand plan to finally spend his life
with Peggy happened in the main timeline,

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:34.980
meaning that there are two Captain Americas
in the MCU.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:39.000
Just after the release of Endgame, Markus
and McFeely sat down with Fandango's Erik

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.870
Davis, who asked if Cap's ending means that,
quote, "an old Captain America was hanging

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:45.620
out this whole time while another Captain
America was saving the day?"

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:49.680
Markus confirmed that he personally rejects
the idea that Steve was ever in an alternate

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:50.680
timeline, saying,

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:54.239
"We are not experts on time travel, but the
Ancient One specifically states that when

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.580
you take an Infinity Stone out of a timeline,
it creates a new timeline.

00:02:57.580 --> 00:03:00.880
So Steve going back and just being there would
not create a new timeline.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.660
So I reject the 'Steve is in an alternate
reality' theory.

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:08.090
I do believe that there is simply a period
in world history from about '48 to now where

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:09.380
there are two Steve Rogers.

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:12.640
And anyway, for a large chunk of that, one
of them is frozen in ice.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:14.599
So it's not like they'd be running into each
other."

00:03:14.599 --> 00:03:18.380
It's hard to say whether the two directors
or the two writers are more accurate here,

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:21.970
since this is all science-fiction, but the
Russos' explanation better aligns with the

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:23.980
rules of time travel established in Endgame.

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:28.200
Even so, Markus and McFeely have said that
Cap's ending is open for interpretation.

00:03:28.209 --> 00:03:32.099
Though he and McFeely prefer their theory
to the Russos', Markus has said that the issue

00:03:32.099 --> 00:03:36.209
of whether Steve's life with Peggy happened
in an alternate reality or in the main MCU

00:03:36.209 --> 00:03:38.860
timeline is, quote, "a question with many
answers."

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:41.100
So, mystery sort of solved?

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:42.220
We'll leave it at that.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:43.540
"It was beautiful."

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:46.380
"I'm happy for you."

